<h3>Keeping your doors open during roof work</h3>
<p>Many commercial projects can proceed while the building stays in use. We plan around peak hours, keep entrances and parking areas clear where possible, mark safe walkways, and talk through noise, odor and dust concerns ahead of time. For sensitive operations, we can phase the work so only one section of the building is affected at a time.</p>

<h2>Weather Considerations for Temple Commercial Roofs</h2>
<p>Central Texas puts commercial roofs through a demanding cycle. Summer heat and UV break down membranes and coatings over time, and dark surfaces absorb heat that ends up in your cooling bills. Spring storms between roughly March and June can bring hail that bruises membranes and dents metal panels, plus straight-line winds that test perimeter edge metal and flashing. Heavy downpours and flash-flood events quickly reveal any drainage weaknesses on a low-slope roof. After a significant storm, see our <a href="/services/storm-damage-roof-repair-temple-tx/">storm damage roof repair</a> page and check the <a href="/weather/">Temple weather page</a> for recent conditions.</p>
<p>Facilities along the I-35 corridor and in industrial areas also tend to have a lot of rooftop equipment. Every HVAC curb, pipe and conduit penetration is a place where flashing must be maintained, and service technicians walking the roof can cause punctures.</p>

<h2>When to Schedule a Commercial Roof Inspection</h2>
<ul>
<li>Twice a year, ideally in spring and fall, to stay ahead of storm seasons</li>
